Android Pentesting Notes — Licensing
====================================

Copyright (c) 2025-2026 FlyPhantomFly and contributors.

This repository is a documentation-plus-code work, and it is licensed as two
things, because it is two things:

  * THE CONTENT — prose, chapters, diagrams, tables, cheat sheets, glossaries,
    lab write-ups, FAQs and every other .md file — is licensed under the
    Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence (CC BY 4.0).

  * THE CODE — every script and source file, whatever its extension: .py, .sh,
    .js, .mjs, .java, .kt, .kts, .c, .h, .cpp, .s, .smali, .aidl, .xml,
    .gradle, .pro, .yml, .json, and the fenced code blocks embedded inside the
    markdown — is licensed under the MIT licence.

  * A SMALL, NAMED SET OF THIRD-PARTY FILES is licensed by its own authors, not
    by this project. Section 3 lists every one of them. Nothing in sections 1
    or 2 applies to those files.

Where the split is unclear for a particular file, the more permissive licence
(MIT) applies. A code block quoted inside a chapter is code; the paragraph
explaining it is content.

If you are just here to use the material: read the chapters, run the scripts,
teach from them, quote them. Keep the attribution, and do not claim you wrote
it. That is the whole of it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
LICENCE STATUS: CONFIRMED BY THE REPOSITORY OWNER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
The CC BY 4.0 (content) + MIT (code) split below was reviewed and confirmed
before first publication. It is the conventional shape for a repository that
is part prose and part tooling.

Two properties of that choice, stated plainly because they are not reversible
in the ordinary way:

  * A CC licence, once granted, cannot be revoked for copies already
    distributed. Re-licensing affects future versions only.
  * Changing the terms becomes materially harder the moment an external
    contribution is merged, because that contributor's work arrives under
    these terms.

Section 3 carves out third-party material that neither licence covers.
Section 4 lists the remaining items to keep an eye on over time.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------


