==========================================================================
3. THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL — NOT COVERED BY SECTIONS 1 AND 2
==========================================================================

This repository is original work with three documented exceptions. Each is a
file captured from a real device for study, committed unmodified so that the
chapter's claims can be checked against it. This project asserts no copyright
over any of them, and neither CC BY 4.0 nor MIT is applied to them.

There is no verbatim reproduction of AOSP SOURCE CODE anywhere in this
repository. The style guide requires short illustrative excerpts only, and that
rule held: the material below is device configuration and policy, not code.

3.1  25-Binder/examples/fixture-device-snapshot/

     A snapshot captured from a physical Xiaomi device. The directory's own
     README.md records how each file was obtained.

     plat_service_contexts.txt
         Derived from AOSP  system/sepolicy/private/service_contexts
         Licence: Apache License 2.0, (c) The Android Open Source Project.
         The build strips the copyright header; line 1 retains the
         "#line 1" provenance marker naming the source file.

     system_ext_service_contexts.txt
         Two parts, two provenances:
           - MIUI/Xiaomi OEM service labels (proprietary, unlicensed).
           - device/qcom/sepolicy/generic/private/service_contexts,
             BSD 3-Clause, (c) 2018 The Linux Foundation. THE COMPLETE
             BSD-3-CLAUSE NOTICE TRAVELS WITH THE FILE AND IS RETAINED
             VERBATIM, as clause 1 of that licence requires.

     product_service_contexts.txt
         device/qcom/sepolicy/qva/product/private/service_contexts,
         BSD 3-Clause, (c) 2018-2020 The Linux Foundation. The complete
         notice is retained verbatim.

     vnd_service_contexts.txt, service_list.txt, ps.txt, getprop.txt
         Runtime output from a device (process table, property dump, service
         list). Factual machine output, no licence header. Third-party
         application names in ps.txt were pseudonymised — see
         72-RealWorldFindings/references/README.md section 4.

3.2  22-SELinux/examples/policy-rules-measured.cil

     65 policy rule lines quoted verbatim from /system/etc/selinux/
     plat_sepolicy.cil on a physical arm64 device, extracted from a 1.98 MB
     file. Derived from AOSP system/sepolicy/, Apache License 2.0,
     (c) The Android Open Source Project. The file's own header states which
     lines are verbatim: every line beginning with "(" is byte-identical to
     the device. The surrounding commentary is original and is CC BY 4.0.

3.3  22-SELinux/examples/seapp_contexts-measured.txt

     39 rule lines, unedited, from /system/etc/selinux/plat_seapp_contexts
     (AOSP system/sepolicy/private/seapp_contexts, Apache License 2.0) and
     /vendor/etc/selinux/vendor_seapp_contexts (MediaTek OEM, proprietary).
     The file's own header names both sources. The commentary is original.

3.4  A note on ABI declarations

     25-Binder/examples/binder-probe.c declares the Linux Binder UAPI
     structures and ioctl macros (binder_write_read, binder_transaction_data,
     flat_binder_object, BINDER_*/BC_*/BR_*), transcribed rather than included
     so the file compiles standalone. These are a functional interface: the
     names and values are necessarily identical to
     include/uapi/linux/android/binder.h because that is what an ABI is. All
     comments and all code around them are original. Linux UAPI headers carry
     the syscall-note exception; no copyright is asserted over the
     declarations themselves.

3.5  Named tools, apps and standards

     Frida, Magisk, JADX, apktool, Ghidra, IDA, radare2, objection, MobSF,
     LSPosed, Xposed, RootBeer, OWASP MASVS/MASTG and the deliberately
     vulnerable training apps (DIVA, OVAA, InsecureBankv2, CMPen, the
     MobileHackingLab targets) are DISCUSSED and CITED here. None of their
     source is vendored into this repository. Each remains under its own
     licence and its own trademarks belong to its own owners. Where a chapter
     shows a script that drives one of these tools, the script is original and
     is MIT.

     OWASP MASVS/MASTG control text is not reproduced. The chapters use the
     control IDENTIFIERS (MASVS-STORAGE, MASVS-CRYPTO, and so on) and then
     paraphrase under this repository's own headings.


==========================================================================
4. IF YOU CHANGE THIS LICENCE
==========================================================================

Section 3 is the part that must survive any relicensing. It is not a courtesy;
the BSD-3-Clause notices in 3.1 are retained because that licence requires it,
and removing them while redistributing the files would be a breach.

Confirmed at first publication, and worth re-checking over time:

  * [CONFIRMED] CC BY 4.0 + MIT is the intended split — not a single licence,
    not share-alike (CC BY-SA 4.0), and not non-commercial. CC BY-NC was
    considered and rejected: it would make the material unusable in paid
    training, which is a legitimate use of a teaching repository.
  * [CONFIRMED] The copyright holder name and year line at the top of this
    file. Note this is an authorship assertion and is deliberately public; it
    is distinct from the anonymisation pass, which removed account, device and
    third-party application identifiers from the chapters.
  * Whether to vendor the full CC BY 4.0 legal code into this file rather than
    incorporating it by reference. Referencing the canonical URL is valid and
    is what Creative Commons recommends; vendoring it makes the repository
    self-contained offline. Do not retype it — copy it from the canonical
    source.
  * That no later contribution has added third-party material without adding a
    row to section 3.
